# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-

def blocks():
    return [
        {"t": "h1", "text": "Day 9 — System design and the lead conversation"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Outcome: you can run a 25-minute design without jumping to Kubernetes, and you can tell four stories from your own eight years using a structure a panel can score."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Time box (4 hours)"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "20 min: memorize the design spine below. It is the outline you write on the board in the first three minutes.",
            "90 min: Design A and Design B, spoken, with a timer. Stop at 20 minutes and summarize.",
            "70 min: write your four stories. Use the sample only as a skeleton. Replace every fact.",
            "40 min: behavioral questions aloud, 2 minutes each.",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "The design spine"},
        {"t": "b", "numbered": True, "items": [
            "Clarify the user, the success metric, the scale you are assuming, and what is out of scope. State assumptions and let them correct you.",
            "Name the API: resources, verbs, sync versus async, status codes.",
            "Name the data: the aggregate, the key, the constraint, the index that matches the hot query.",
            "Name consistency: what is in one SQL transaction, what is a message, what may lag.",
            "Name failure: timeout, retry, duplicate, poison message, and what the user sees.",
            "Name operation: logs, the one dashboard, deploy and rollback, identities and SQL roles.",
            "Name the tradeoff you refused, and the first slice you would ship.",
        ]},
        {"t": "lead", "title": "Scale numbers", "text": "If they do not give numbers, assume some and say them. For example: 50 requests a second peak, 2 million orders a year, p95 under 300 milliseconds for the read, email within a minute. Invite correction. Design changes when the number changes. A lead who designs for a million events a second without being asked will overbuild and run out of time."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Design A — Order API"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Prompt: design the backend for creating an order, viewing it, and emailing a confirmation. Partners retry. Two customers must not lose updates. You are the tech lead."},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "POST /orders accepts a command and an Idempotency-Key. The server prices the order. The client does not send IsPriority or Total as authority. Response is 201 with a body and Location, or 200 with the original body if the key repeats.",
            "The write is one SQL transaction: order, lines, idempotency row, outbox row. Unique constraint on the idempotency key and on any business key. rowversion on the order. PATCH returns 409 on a conflict.",
            "GET /orders/{id} is a no-tracking projection authorized by tenant and policy. List uses a keyset and an index on tenant, created time, and id.",
            "A relay publishes OrderConfirmed. A function sends email. The consumer deduplicates. The user does not wait on the mail provider. Status is visible on the order.",
            "Host the API on App Service with slots, managed identity, and a runtime SQL role. The function has its own identity and only the rights it needs. Migration identity is separate.",
            "Timeouts and a resilience policy on any sync dependency such as a tax service. If tax is down, decide with the business: reject the order, or accept PendingTax. Say which you recommend and why.",
            "Ship the synchronous create and the outbox before you split a pricing microservice. Pricing can be a module.",
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Close with: the risk I would watch in week one is a duplicate email from redelivery, so the unique message id ships in the first increment, not later."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Design B — Nightly file of a million rows"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Prompt: a partner drops a CSV in storage every night. You must validate, load, and report failures. The HTTP API must stay responsive."},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "A blob trigger or an Event Grid event starts a function that only records the file and enqueues a control message. It does not parse a million rows inside one invocation.",
            "A splitter reads the file in chunks and enqueues chunk messages with a file id and a byte range or row range. Workers on a Premium plan or a dedicated plan upsert rows.",
            "Each chunk commits with a row-range idempotency key so a retry does not double-insert. Failures of a row go to an error table with the line number and the reason. The file has a state: Received, Processing, CompletedWithErrors, Failed.",
            "SQL uses bulk copy or a staged table and a set-based merge for speed. EF SaveChanges per row will not make the window. Say that plainly.",
            "The API serves status and error pages from SQL. It does not handle the file on a request thread.",
            "An alert fires if the file has not reached a terminal state by a business deadline. A poison chunk dead-letters without stopping the other chunks, and the file state shows the gap.",
            "Identity: the worker's managed identity can read the container and write the staging schema. It cannot alter the schema.",
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Close with: I would prove chunk retry on a 10 MB file before I talk about scale, and I would show the error CSV the partner can fetch."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Design C — if they ask multi-tenant"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Default: one database, TenantId on every tenant-owned row, a global query filter, a unique index that includes TenantId, and the tenant id taken from the token.",
            "The API authorizes the route, and a test proves tenant A cannot read tenant B's id.",
            "A noisy or regulated tenant can move to a separate database. The application resolves the connection by tenant. Migrations must apply to every database, which is an operational cost I name before I offer the option.",
            "I do not start with a database per tenant for hundreds of small customers. The migration and the connection story will dominate the team.",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Behavioral structure"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Use Situation, Task, Action, Result, and a last line: what the team does now. Keep it under two minutes. I is for your decision. We is for the work the team did. Panels listen for whether you actually decided something."},
        {"t": "warn", "title": "Sample stories are scaffolds", "text": "The next three stories are examples of shape and detail. Do not memorize them as your career. Replace the system, the number, and the decision with yours. A panel that hears a generic checkout story and then asks a follow-up you cannot answer will trust you less than if you had told a smaller true story."},
        {"t": "h3", "text": "Sample — production incident"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Situation: a release went out at 11:00 and by 11:20 the order GET p95 was above four seconds. Task: I was the tech lead on call and had to choose rollback or fix-forward. Action: I checked the deploy marker against the latency chart, saw SQL dependency time jump, and swapped back to the previous slot within fifteen minutes because the migration had only added a nullable column and the old code was safe. Then I looked at Query Store. A new screen was loading lines with a query that scanned Orders. We added a covering index in a reviewed script, load-tested the read, and shipped it that afternoon. Result: latency returned to about 200 milliseconds after the swap, and the index kept the new screen. The team rule now is that a list endpoint PR includes the filter, the index, and a sample plan, and rollback is pre-written on the release ticket."},
        {"t": "h3", "text": "Sample — disagreement"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Situation: a senior developer wanted a generic repository and a new microservice for pricing before a release. Task: I needed a data-access approach the whole team could review, and a date we could hit. Action: I asked what change we were protecting. We had one query shape and one team. I proposed we keep pricing in a module, inject a focused IPriceService, and skip the generic repository so the SQL stayed visible. I wrote a one-page note with the consequences and I asked them to own the module boundary so the later split would be possible. Result: we shipped on time, the query stayed in one class we could tune, and the relationship held because they kept a real design ownership. I would split pricing later if a second team needed to release it."},
        {"t": "h3", "text": "Sample — growing someone"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Situation: a developer with two years of experience was copying controller code and calling SaveChanges from actions. Task: I needed the next feature to land in a shape the team could maintain. Action: I paired on one endpoint, moved the use case into a handler, and left them to do the next one with a checklist: cancellation token, a projection for the read, a test for the failure path, and a status code. I reviewed the rule, not the formatting. Result: they owned the returns API two months later and caught an over-posting bug in someone else's PR. The checklist is now in the repo."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Tell me about a time you missed a date.",
         "a": "Pick a real date. Say the original commitment, the new risk you learned, and the day you told stakeholders, which should be early. Say what you cut: a report, an edge case, a non-critical admin screen. Say what you refused to cut: auth, a migration rollback, or the payment test. Say the new date you hit and the planning change, such as estimating the first slice only. Avoid a story where you heroically worked a weekend and changed nothing about planning.",
         "lead": "The score is whether you re-planned in public. Quietly absorbing the slip is what a strong individual contributor does. A lead changes the scope with the stakeholders while the date still means something."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you handle technical debt?",
         "a": "I name the debt in user or operator language: this list endpoint will time out at the next tenant's volume, or this Framework host is out of support in November. I attach a cost to leave it and a cost to fix it. I put a slice of the fix in the same increment as a nearby feature when the code is already open. I keep a small reserve, and I spend it on the item with the nearest incident, not on the rewrite I find interesting. I do not hide a migration inside an unrelated story. I also delete debt. A pattern we no longer need is a ticket with an owner.",
         "lead": "Bring one debt item from your current system, with the incident it implies and the smallest fix. Vague passion about clean code is weaker than one dated risk."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you review code as a lead without becoming the bottleneck?",
         "a": "I publish review rules so people can apply them without me: cancellation, authorization, SQL shape, secrets, tests for the money path, and migration safety. I review the contract and the risk, and I let the formatter own the braces. I am available at agreed times so reviews do not sit overnight. I ask the author to walk me through a design before a thousand-line PR exists. Two approvals are required for auth and for schema. A junior's first reviews are paired so the comments teach. If every PR waits for me, I have failed, and I pick a second reviewer and a written rule to remove myself from the middle.",
         "lead": "Say the rule you would write down this month. A panel believes a lead who has a rule. It is less convinced by a lead who says I review everything carefully."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What do you do when you disagree with your manager's technical direction?",
         "a": "I restate their goal until they agree I understood it. I put my alternative in writing with the risk, the date, and the operational cost. I distinguish a reversible choice from a one-way door. On a reversible choice I disagree, commit, and watch the metric we named. On a one-way door, such as a shared database across a boundary we cannot undo, or a production login as db_owner, I say that clearly and offer a smaller experiment. I do not relitigate in the team channel after the decision. If the decision is unsafe for customers or staff, I escalate with the risk written down, not with a complaint.",
         "lead": "The one-way door language is the technique. Use it once in the interview, on a real example, and then stop. Repeating the phrase sounds rehearsed."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Questions they ask you, which you should prepare"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "What is the first slice you would want me to lead in the first 90 days?",
            "Which systems are still on .NET Framework, and is there a funded plan before support ends?",
            "How does a change reach production? Who can roll it back at 21:00?",
            "What does on-call look like, and what woke the team up last month?",
            "Where does the team want a tech lead to spend time: design, review, people, or still a large share of coding?",
            "How are SQL changes reviewed, and what rights does the application identity have today?",
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "A lead who asks about rollback, identity, and on-call sounds like they intend to live with the system. Ask two questions, not six, at the end of a round."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Evening drill"},
        {"t": "b", "numbered": True, "items": [
            "Whiteboard Design A in 20 minutes. Photograph it. Look for a missing failure mode.",
            "Record your incident story. Delete any sentence that does not contain a decision or a number.",
            "Write the 90-day focus you want, in five lines, so you can answer if they ask.",
        ]},
        {"t": "break"},
    ]
